'''John Bernard Larroquette'''<ref>{{Cite web |title=John Larroquette |url=https://www.tvguide.com/celebrities/john-larroquette/credits/3000414394/ |access-date=2022-03-04 |magazine=TV Guide |language=en}}</ref> ({{IPAc-en|ˌ|l|ær|ə|'|k|ɛ|t}}; born November 25, 1947) is an American actor. He is known for his starring roles in the [[NBC]] military drama series ''[[Baa Baa Black Sheep (TV series)|Baa Baa Black Sheep]]'' (1976–1978), the NBC sitcom ''[[Night Court]]'' (1984–1992; [[Night Court (2023 TV series)|2023–2025]]) for which he received four consecutive [[Primetime Emmy Award]]s wins for [[Primetime Emmy Award for Outstanding Supporting Actor in a Comedy Series|Outstanding Supporting Actor in a Comedy Series]] during the earlier incarnation, the NBC sitcom ''[[The John Larroquette Show]]'' (1993–1996), the [[David E. Kelley]] legal drama series ''[[The Practice (TV series)|The Practice]]'' (1997–2002), the [[American Broadcasting Company|ABC]] legal comedy-drama series ''[[Boston Legal]]'' (2004–2008), and the [[TNT (American TV network)|TNT]] series ''[[The Librarians (2014 TV series)|The Librarians]]'' (2014–2018).

In 2011, he made his [[Broadway (theatre)|Broadway]] debut in the musical revival of [[Frank Loesser]]'s ''[[How to Succeed in Business Without Really Trying (musical)|How to Succeed in Business Without Really Trying]]'' alongside [[Daniel Radcliffe]]. He played J. B. Bigley in a role for which he received a [[Tony Award for Best Featured Actor in a Musical]], and a [[Drama Desk Award for Outstanding Featured Actor in a Musical]]. The following year he starred as William Russell in the Broadway revival of [[Gore Vidal]]'s ''[[The Best Man (play)|The Best Man]]'' (2012) directed by [[Mike Nichols]] starring [[James Earl Jones]], [[Candice Bergen]], and [[Angela Lansbury]].

He made his film debut by providing the opening narration of the horror film ''[[The Texas Chain Saw Massacre]]'' (1974), following which he appeared in films such as ''[[Stripes (film)|Stripes]]'' (1981), ''[[Choose Me]]'' (1984), ''[[Blind Date (1987 film)|Blind Date]]'' (1987), ''[[Madhouse (1990 film)|Madhouse]]'' (1990), ''[[Richie Rich (film)|Richie Rich]]'' (1994), and the [[Hallmark Channel]] mystery series ''[[McBride (film series)|McBride]]'' (2005–2008).

==Early life==
Larroquette was born in [[New Orleans]], [[Louisiana]], on November 25, 1947, the son of Berthalla Oramous, a [[department store]] clerk, and John Edgar Larroquette Jr., who was in the United States Navy.<ref>{{cite web |title=John Larroquette |url=https://www.tcm.com/tcmdb/person/108933%7C0/John-Larroquette/#biography |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120618164859/http://www.tcm.com/tcmdb/person/108933%7C0/John-Larroquette/#biography |url-status=dead |archive-date=June 18, 2012 |website=[[Turner Classic Movies]] |access-date=August 30, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Xe1ZBAAAQBAJ|title=The Odd Couple on Stage and Screen: A History with Cast and Crew Profiles and an Episode Guide|last=Leszczak|first=Bob|page=77|date=2014-08-23|publisher=McFarland|isbn=9781476615394|language=en}}</ref> His paternal grandfather, John (Jean Edgar) Larroquette Sr., was born in [[France]] (1881) and emigrated to the United States in 1895.

Larroquette grew up in the [[Ninth Ward of New Orleans]], near the [[French Quarter]]. He played [[clarinet]] and [[saxophone]] through childhood and into high school. He attended [[Holy Cross School (New Orleans)|Holy Cross School]] through his sophomore year before involuntarily moving to Francis T. Nicholls High School to finish his secondary education.<ref name="JL-Edu">{{cite web |title=John Larroquette arrives in his hometown to receive honorary UNO degree|url=https://www.nola.com/entertainment_life/movies_tv/john-larroquette-arrives-in-his-hometown-to-receive-honorary-uno-degree/article_5042665e-1f8a-5bed-b262-4dceaea8f0f7.html|website=nola.com|date=May 17, 2013|access-date=January 22, 2024}}</ref> It was his senior year at Nicholls High School that he first discovered acting. He was offered a scholarship to [[Louisiana State University]] after winning a state high school speech title but decided not to attend the university.<ref name="JL-Edu"/> He served for a time in the [[United States Navy Reserve]].<ref>{{cite web |title=John Larroquette Biography |url=https://www.tvguide.com/celebrities/john-larroquette/bio/3000414394/ |website=TV Guide |publisher=Fandom |access-date=August 2, 2026}}</ref>

==Career==
===Early career===
His first acting role in Hollywood was providing the opening [[voiceover]] narration for ''[[The Texas Chain Saw Massacre]]'' (1974). Larroquette did this as a favor for the film's director [[Tobe Hooper]], and was paid in [[Cannabis (drug)|marijuana]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Wright |first=Tracy |date=2023-01-16 |title=John Larroquette was paid in marijuana to narrate 'Texas Chainsaw Massacre,' talks 'Night Court' reboot |url=https://www.foxnews.com/entertainment/john-larroquette-paid-marijuana-narrate-texas-chainsaw-massacre |access-date=2024-11-01 |website=Fox News |language=en-US}}</ref> His first series regular role was in the 1970s [[NBC]] program ''[[Baa Baa Black Sheep (TV series)|Baa Baa Black Sheep]]'', where he portrayed a [[World War II]] [[United States Marine Corps]] fighter pilot, 2nd Lt. Bob Anderson.

In a 1975 appearance on ''[[Sanford and Son]]'', Larroquette played Lamont's counterpart in a fictitious sitcom based on Fred and Lamont called "Steinberg and Son". During the filming of ''[[Stripes (film)|Stripes]]'' (1981), his nose was nearly cut off in an accident. He ran down a hall into a door that was supposed to open but did not, and his head went through the window in the door.<ref name="20q">"20 Questions", ''Playboy'', April 1990.</ref>

===''Night Court'' (1984&ndash;1992)===
[[Image:John Larroquette.jpg|thumb|right|upright|Larroquette attending the [[Emmy Award]]s in 1988]]
Larroquette played Assistant District Attorney [[Dan Fielding]] on ''[[Night Court]]''; the character was initially rather conservative, but changed after the sitcom's creator [[Reinhold Weege]] came to learn more about Larroquette's sense of humor.<ref name=Star/><ref name=Broadway/> The role won him [[Emmy Award]]s in 1985, 1986, 1987 and 1988. In 1989, he asked not to be considered for an Emmy Award.<ref name="avcrr">{{cite journal| first=Nathan| last=Rabin| url=http://www.avclub.com/content/feature/random_roles_john_larroquette/2| website=AV Club| title=Random Roles: John Larroquette| date=June 5, 2008| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080704195959/http://www.avclub.com/content/feature/random_roles_john_larroquette/2| archive-date=July 4, 2008}}</ref> His four consecutive wins were, at the time, a record.

''Night Court'' ran on [[NBC]] from 1984 until 1992. Larroquette, [[Harry Anderson]] (as Judge Harry Stone), and [[Richard Moll]] (as bailiff Bull Shannon) appeared in every episode of the series. There was talk a spin-off show revolving around Dan Fielding, but Larroquette rejected the idea.<ref name=Star/> Later, Larroquette ended up on the 2023 series continuation as the only regular character from the original in the revival. On February 13, 2024, while talking to reporters, Larroquette admitted feeling a little sad when he first walked on the set of the ''Night Court'' revival, due to being one of the only living cast members from the original run. But as time went on, he started feeling better and credited co-star/executive producer [[Melissa Rauch]] for rejuvenating the franchise.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2024/02/night-court-star-john-larroquette-addresses-rebooting-comedy-1235824318/|title='Night Court' Star John Larroquette Says Making The Reboot Made Him Sad … At First|work=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|first=Lynette|last=Rice|access-date=February 13, 2024|date=February 13, 2024}}</ref>

===''The John Larroquette Show''===
Instead of a spin-off, Larroquette and [[Don Reo]] developed a show revolving around some of Larroquette's own personal demons, particularly alcoholism. ''[[The John Larroquette Show]]'', named by the insistence of NBC, starred Larroquette as the character John Hemingway. The show was lauded by critics, but failed to attract the prime-time audience, ranking around number 97 for most of the first season.

NBC threatened cancellation; however, Larroquette and Reo were granted the chance to retool the series, which saw it carry on for just over two more seasons, ending during its fourth season after 84 episodes, the last six of which were not aired. The show has a loyal cult following, although the series has never received an official home video release from Warner Bros.

===''Boston Legal'' and other television roles===
In 1998, he guest-starred on three episodes of the [[legal drama]] ''[[The Practice]]''. His portrayal of [[Joey Heric]], a wealthy, wisecracking, [[narcissism|narcissistic]] [[psychopathy|psychopath]] with a habit of stabbing his [[gay]] lovers to death, won him his fifth Emmy Award. He reprised the role for one episode in 2002, for which he was once again nominated for an Emmy Award. He also appeared in an episode of ''[[The West Wing]]'' as [[White House Counsel]] Lionel Tribbey.

In 2003, Larroquette reprised his narration for the remake of ''[[The Texas Chainsaw Massacre (2003 film)|The Texas Chainsaw Massacre]]''. From 2004 to 2006, he played the title role in the ''[[McBride (TV series)|McBride]]'' series of ten ''Hallmark Mysteries'' television films. In 2007, he joined the cast of ''[[Boston Legal]]'' playing [[Carl Sack]], a serious, ethical lawyer. He also guest-starred in the drama ''[[House (TV series)|House]]'' where he played a previously catatonic father awakened to try to save his son.

From 2014 to 2018, Larroquette was a regular on ''[[The Librarians (2014 TV series)|The Librarians]]'' as Jenkins (actually the long-lived Camelot knight [[Galahad|Sir Galahad]]), who provides support to the Librarians as a researcher and caretaker.<ref>{{cite web| last=Ausiello| first=Michael| date=2014-04-10| title=TNT Orders Librarians Series Starring Rebecca Romijn, Leverage Fave; Noah Wyle to Recur| url=https://www.tvline.com/news/the-librarians-tv-series-tnt-rebecca-romijn-noah-wyle-508724/| access-date=2020-10-02| website=TVLine| language=en-US}}</ref>

===Film===
His starring roles include the 1989 film ''[[Second Sight (1989 film)|Second Sight]]'' with [[Bronson Pinchot]], and the 1990 film ''[[Madhouse (1990 film)|Madhouse]]'' with [[Kirstie Alley]]. Other films in which Larroquette had significant roles include: ''[[Stripes (film)|Stripes]]'' (1981), ''[[Blind Date (1987 film)|Blind Date]]'' (1987), ''[[Star Trek III: The Search for Spock]]'' (1984), ''[[Choose Me]]'' (1984), ''[[Meatballs Part II]]'' (1984), ''[[Summer Rental]]'' (1985), ''[[JFK (film)|JFK]]'' (1991), and ''[[Richie Rich (film)|Richie Rich]]'' (1994).

===Theatre===
In 1975 he portrayed the lead role in the comedic play ''Tarboosh!'' at the Studio Playhouse in Los Angeles.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Drake |first1=Sylvie |title='Tarboosh!' at Studio Playhouse |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-los-angeles-times-tarboosh/185954223/ |website=The Los Angeles Times}}</ref>

Larroquette made his musical stage debut in the Los Angeles production of ''[[How the Grinch Stole Christmas!]]'' as Old Max in 2009. He made his [[Broadway theatre|Broadway]] debut in the 2011 revival of ''[[How to Succeed in Business Without Really Trying (musical)|How to Succeed in Business Without Really Trying]]'' as J B. Biggley alongside [[Daniel Radcliffe]].<ref name=Star/> He won the [[Tony Award for Best Featured Actor in a Musical]] and the [[Drama Desk Award for Outstanding Featured Actor in a Musical]] for his performance in the show.<ref>{{cite journal| url=http://broadwayworld.com/article/2011-Tony-Nominations-Announced-THE-BOOK-OF-MORMON-Leads-With-14-20110503| title=2011 Tony Nominations Announced! THE BOOK OF MORMON Leads With 14!| first=Robert| last=Diamond| date=May 3, 2011| journal=Broadway World| access-date=October 28, 2021}}</ref>

He appeared on Broadway in a revival of [[Gore Vidal]]'s ''[[The Best Man (play)|The Best Man]]'', starring [[James Earl Jones]], [[Angela Lansbury]], and [[Candice Bergen]]. In early 2019, he was back in New York City starring in the play ''Nantucket Sleigh Ride'' by John Guare, at the Lincoln Center Theatre. In this off-beat play, Larroquette portrayed the lead character, Edmund "Mundie" Gowery, for a three-month run.

==Personal life==
Larroquette met his wife Elizabeth Ann Cookson in 1974 while working in the play ''[[Enter Laughing]]''. They married on July 4, 1975, as that was the only day they had off from rehearsals.<ref name=Broadway>{{cite web|url=http://www.broadway.com/shows/how-succeed-business-without-really-trying/buzz/155580/john-larroquette-on-succeeding-on-broadway-and-looking-down-on-daniel-radcliffe| title=John Larroquette on Succeeding on Broadway and Looking Down on Daniel Radcliffe| first=Kathy| last=Henderson| work=[[John Gore Organization|Broadway.com]]| access-date=June 4, 2018}}</ref> Cookson had a daughter, Lisa, from a previous relationship, and she and Larroquette would have two sons together, Jonathan and Benjamin.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://hollywoodmask.com/p/john-larroquette-wife.html | title=Inside John Larroquette and Wife Elizabeth Larroquette's Decades-Long Marriage | date=April 25, 2023 }}</ref> Jonathan co-hosts a comedy podcast called ''[[Uhh Yeah Dude]]''.

Larroquette battled alcoholism from the mid-1970s to the early 1980s. On ''[[The Tonight Show with Jay Leno]]'' on March 10, 2007, he joked, "I was known to have a cocktail or sixty." He stopped drinking on February 6, 1982.<ref name=Star>{{cite news| url=https://www.thestar.com/entertainment/article/963442--john-larroquette-this-is-a-dark-ride| title=John Larroquette: This is a Dark Ride| first=Richard| last=Ouzounian| author-link=Richard Ouzounian| newspaper=[[Toronto Star]]| date=January 4, 2011| access-date=April 2, 2011}}</ref>

